From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 08:02:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Prefetch the records of a cursor's next steps

Design decision 10. Each cursor step was a round trip to the backend and
a statement, about 1.1 ms on the DAYU200 even after the store rows were
kept in the actor. A cursor's request to the backend now asks for more
records than the one it finds: 1 at first, then twice as many as the
cursor's last request, up to 64, as Gecko and Chromium prefetch; but 1
after a write to its object store, for a step with a key, and always for
"nextunique" over a non-unique index, whose later keys the backend finds
only by reading every record of each key. The backend has returned the
records that the next steps without a key find after the found one since
the index cursors; it now returns them only while the stored bytes of
all the records it returns (keys, primary keys and values) stay within
1 MiB, leaving out the record that would cross it before reading its
value. The found record comes back whatever its size.

The cursor keeps those records and takes continue(), continue(key),
continuePrimaryKey() and advance() steps from them (step 9 of "iterate a
cursor" on the kept records) as long as they hold the record that the
step finds and no request that writes to the cursor's object store (put,
add, delete, clear, cursor update and delete) and no schema operation was
made in the transaction since the request that returned them was made;
writes to the transaction's other object stores leave them current.
Writes are counted when they are made, so a write made after a cursor's
request but before its result arrived counts too. Otherwise the cursor
drops them and asks the backend. A step taken from them is still an
entry of the transaction's request list, delivered in request order in
its own task. A cursor keeps no records once its transaction can't take
more steps, and the transaction drops the records of its cursors when it
finishes.

The fork test cursor-prefetch.any.js checks every step of cursors taking
turns against a model of the spec's steps, with seeded random steps and
writes through the store and the cursors, and the cases that a stale
record would get wrong, also with writes to another store between the
steps. The backend test test_cursor_steps_stop_at_the_bytes_limit pins
the byte bound.

Validation: Host: `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits` 144 passed, 1 skipped; WPT tests/wpt/tests/IndexedDB, 8 processes, SpiderMonkey 4,466 and 4,466, QuickJS 4,466 and 4,465 of 4,511 (P4.1 4,465 / 4,465; the differences are the known worker/sharedworker and origin-isolation flips, no unexpected results but QuickJS's idbversionchangeevent.any.worker open.blocked once); /_mozilla/IndexedDB/ with the interface lists 261 of 261 on SM, 259 of 261 on QuickJS (the interface lists, expected FAIL); cursor-prefetch.any.js 19 subtests pass with this build and with the P4.1 build, and fail with builds that ignore writes when taking kept records, stamp them when the reply arrives, or mis-handle continue(key) on them. DAYU200 (QuickJS + RDB, checked-release, 2 processes, idle host): WPT IndexedDB/ 4,448 of 4,511 (P4.1 4,441); interleaved-cursors-large passes in all three globals in 48.9-57.4 s (P4.1 TIMEOUT at 60 s), interleaved-cursors-small 21.7-24.3 s (26.3-29.0 s), idbcursor_iterating 1.8-2.2 s (3.8-4.4 s); the unexpected results are file_support (send_keys), idb-partitioned-coverage (testharness bookkeeping in its iframe, P4.2 report), idbversionchangeevent.any.worker, and createObjectStore-exception-order.any.sharedworker and rename-store.any.worker, which pass 3 of 3 alone; /_mozilla/IndexedDB/ 259 of 259; `./mach test-unit --ohos -p servo-storage` RDB only 145 passed / 3 skipped, SQLite and RDB 259 / 4. Probe page of 2,000 records, P4.1 vs this series: a store cursor step 1.73 -> 0.26 ms, an index cursor step 1.95 -> 0.29 ms, a key cursor step 1.53 -> 0.18 ms, two cursors taking turns 1.27 -> 0.23 ms, prevunique over 20 keys of 100 records 2.85 -> 1.45 ms, nextunique over 2 keys of 1,000 records 3.2 -> 2.5 ms. `./mach fmt`, `./mach test-tidy` (only the known libsqlite3-sys advisory), `./mach clippy --locked -- -- --deny warnings`, `RUSTFLAGS="-D warnings" ./mach build --use-crown --locked --profile checked-release` and `./mach check --ohos -- -p servo-storage --all-targets` with SQLite, RDB and both pass. P4 review fixes, folded in and checked at the end of the series (P4.3): the backend test test_cursor_steps_stop_at_the_bytes_limit passes on SQLite (host) and RDB (DAYU200) and fails with the former cap (it returned the record over the limit after the found one, and every large key of a key-only step); host nextest 145 passed / 1 skipped, DAYU200 RDB only 146 / 3, SQLite and RDB 261 / 4; cursor-prefetch.any.js 20 subtests (the new one writes to another store between the steps) pass on host SpiderMonkey, QuickJS and the board; board durations after the fixes: idbcursor_iterating 1.80-2.15 s, interleaved-cursors-small 21.9-23.7 s, interleaved-cursors-large 55.7-56.8 s (before them 1.94-2.23, 22.0-24.7 and 56.3-59.4 s).
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; implemented the prefetch and the fork test, checked the test against mutated builds, measured on host and board; folded in the P4 review fixes: the byte bound on a step's records, write counts per object store, kept records dropped when the transaction finishes)
